## Part 3: Stepped shaft with double cone

### Sketch
- A closed profile drawn on the front plane, sitting on the X axis (the axis of revolution).
- Overall length **400.00 mm**.
- The other dimensions shown in the sketch are 120.00, 75.00, 60.00 (two), 40.00, 15.00 (two), 62.50 (two), 35.00 (two) and 20.00 mm. These set the length and diameter of each step, the width of the middle cone section, and the V-shaped dip between the two raised blocks.

![Part 3 sketch](images/part3_sketch.png)

### Steps
1. Draw the closed profile on the front plane with the bottom edge on the X axis.
2. Add dimensions and constraints until the sketch is fully defined.
3. Use Create > Revolve, select the profile, and choose the X axis as the axis.
4. Revolve through 360°.

### 3D model
A stepped round shaft with a flared double-cone section in the middle, narrowing to a smaller diameter at both ends.

![Part 3 3D model](images/part3_model.png)

---

## Notes
- Every sketch was fully dimensioned from the reference drawing, which is the main skill this task practises.
- Extrude suits parts built from flat profiles (Parts 1 and 2); Revolve suits round parts like shafts (Part 3).
- Using one sketch with a pattern of holes around a construction circle keeps the holes evenly spaced.
- All three parts use mm units, as in the drawings.
- What this task covers: 2D sketching with dimensions and constraints, extrude, cut, hole features, revolve, and reading machine part drawings.
